<li>Author: M. Nagaprasanna / Bench: M. Nagaprasanna</li>
<li>1Reserved on : 14.03.2024Pronounced on : 22.04.2024 R IN THE HIGH COURT OF KARNATAKA AT BENGALURU DATED THIS THE 22ND DAY OF APRIL, 2024 BEFORE THE HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE M. NAGAPRASANNA WRIT PETITION No.483 OF 2023 (GM-RES)BETWEEN:MR. FAROOQ ALI KHANS/O GULZAR ALI KHANAGED ABOUT 56 YEARSRESIDING AT: NO.21BENSON 'A', CROSS ROADBENSON TOWN POSTBENGALURU - 560 046.PROMOTER AND SUSPENDED DIRECTORASSOCIATE DECOR LIMITED. ";}i:2;i:1;}i:3;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"entity";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"...";}i:2;i:564;}i:4;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1854:" PETITIONER(BY SRI. S.BASAVARAJ, SENIOR ADVOCATE FOR SRI. SIVARAMAKRISHNAN M. S., ADVOCATE)AND:1. PUNJAB NATIONAL BANK (FORMERLY KNOWN AS ORIENTAL BANK OF COMMERCE) HAVING ITS CORPORATE BANKING BRANCH AT: MAKER TOWER 'F' WING, CUFFE PARADE MUMBAI - 400 005. 2 REPRESENTED BY ITS DGM.2. UNION BANK OF INDIA STRESSED ASSETS MANAGEMENT VERTICAL BRANCH: THE EAGLE'S FLIGHT 3RD FLOOR, 301-302 SUREN ROAD ANDHERI-KURLA ROAD ANDHERI (EAST) MUMBAI - 400 093. REPRESENTED BY ITS DGM.3. BANK OF BARODA STRESSED ASSETS MANAGEMENT BRANCH 1ST FLOOR, 17/B HORNIMAN CIRCLE FORT, MUMBAI - 400 001. REPRESENTED BY ITS DGM.4. MR. ALOK KAILASH SAKSENA RESOLUTION PROFESSIONAL OF ASSOCIATE DECOR LIMITED (COMPANY UNDER CORPORATE INSOLVENCY RESOLUTION PROCESS) C/O DESAI SAKSENA AND ASSOCIATES CHARTERED ACCOUNTANTS HAVING ITS OFFICE AT: 1ST FLOOR LAXMI BUILDING, SIR PHIROZSHAH MEHTA ROAD, FORT MUMBAI - 400 001.5. MOHAMMED ENTERPRISES (TANZANIA ) LTD., SUCCESSFUL RESOLUTION APPLICANT (SRA) OF ASSOCIATE DECOR LTD., HAVING ITS REGISTERED OFFICE AT: 3 TEXTILE HOUSE, MOROGORO ROAD/INDIRA GANDHI STREET GROUND FLOOR, ILALA DISTRICT P.O BOX 20660, DAR ES SALAAM TANZANIA, REP. BY ITS AUTHORIZED SIGNATORY.6. INSOLVENCY AND BANKRUPTCY BOARD OF INDIA (IBBI) HEAD OFFICE AT: 7TH FLOOR MAYUR BHAWAN, SHANKAR MARKET CONNAUGHT CIRCUS NEW DELHI - 110 001. REP. BY ITS CHAIRPERSON.7. INDIAN INSTITUTE OF INSOLVENCY PROFESSIONALS OF ICAI HEAD OFFICE AT: ICAI BHAWAN 8TH FLOOR, HOSTEL BLOCK, A - 29 SECTOR - 62, NOIDA, U.P - 201 309. REP. BY ITS CHIARMAN.8. MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY THROUGH DEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE (DPIIT) HEAD OFFICE AT: UDYOG BHAWAN NEW DELHI - 110 011. REP. BY ITS SECRETARY9. COMPETITION COMMISSION OF INDIA (CCI) HEAD OFFICE AT: 9TH FLOOR OFFICE BLOCK - 1, KIDWAI NAGAR (EAST) NEW DELHI - 110 023. REP. BY ITS CHAIRPERSON. ";}i:2;i:567;}i:5;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"entity";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"...";}i:2;i:2421;}i:6;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:833:" RESPONDENTS(BY SRI. S.S.NAGANAND, SENIOR ADVOCATE FOR SRI. LOMESH KIRAN N., ADVOCATE FOR R1 TO R3; 4 SMT. LAKSHMY IYENGAR, SENIOR ADVOCATE FOR SRI. AJAY RAO, ADVOCATE FOR R4; SRI. SAJJAN POOVAYYA, SENIOR ADVOCATE FOR SRI. M.NIKILESH RAO, ADVOCATE FOR R5; SRI. TUSHAR MEHTA, SOLICITOR GENERAL OF INDIA REPRESENTING THE UNION OF INDIA) THIS WRIT PETITION IS FILED UNDER ARTICLES 226 AND227 OF THE CONSTITUTION OF INDIA PRAYING TO QUASH ANDSET ASIDE THE MINUTES OF THE 2ND (SECOND) ADJOURNED 19TH(NINETEENTH) MEETING OF THE COMMITTEE OF CREDITORS OFASSOCIATE DECOR LIMITED HELD ON 11.02.2020 AT MUMBAI,MAHARASHTRA (AT ANNEXURE-F) AS NON-EST AND ILLEGAL INEYES OF LAW AND ETC., THIS WRIT PETITION HAVING BEEN HEARD AND RESERVEDFOR ORDERS ON 14.03.2024, COMING ON FOR PRONOUNCEMENTTHIS DAY, THE COURT MADE THE FOLLOWING:- ORDER
<p title=";}i:2;i:2424;}i:7;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3257;}i:8;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:3258;}i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3262;}i:10;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:3263;}i:11;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3279;}i:12;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:3280;}i:13;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3285;}i:14;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:32:">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:3286;}i:15;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3318;}i:16;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:3319;}i:17;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3323;}i:18;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:3324;}i:19;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3340;}i:20;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:3341;}i:21;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3346;}i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:775:">The petitioner is before this Court seeking a slew of prayers and in effect seeking to quash minutes of the 22nd meeting of the Committee of Creditors of Associate D&eacute;cor Limited held on 21-12-2022 as non est and illegal and other prayers are sequel to the said prayer. This Court, accepting certain of the prayers of the petitioner had allowed the writ petition in part, in terms of its order dated 21-11-2023. Subsequent developments took place and while answering review petitions, this Court recalled the order so passed 5 on 21-11-2023 in Review Petition No.573 of 2023 c/w 574 of 2023 in terms of its order dated 28-02-2024. Therefore, the matters are heard afresh, in the light of the order passed in the review petitions on 28-02-2024.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:3347;}i:23;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4122;}i:24;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:4123;}i:25;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4127;}i:26;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:4128;}i:27;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4144;}i:28;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:4145;}i:29;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4150;}i:30;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:529:">2. Heard Sri S. Basavaraj, learned senior counsel for Sri Sivaramakrishnan M.S., learned counsel for the petitioner; Sri S.S.Naganand, learned senior counsel for Sri Lomesh Kiran N., learned counsel for respondent Nos.1 to 3; Smt. Lakshmy Iyengar, learned senior counsel for Sri Ajay Rao, learned counsel for respondent No.4; Sri Sajjan Poovayya, learned senior counsel for Sri M. Nikilesh Rao, learned counsel for respondent No.5 and Sri Tushar Mehta, learned Solicitor General of India for respondent Nos.6 to 9.</p>
<p title=";}i:2;i:4151;}i:31;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4680;}i:32;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:4681;}i:33;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4685;}i:34;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:4686;}i:35;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4702;}i:36;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:4703;}i:37;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4708;}i:38;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:32:">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:4709;}i:39;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4741;}i:40;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:4742;}i:41;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4746;}i:42;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:4747;}i:43;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4763;}i:44;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:4764;}i:45;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4769;}i:46;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1154:">3. The facts, in brief, adumbrated are as follows:- Between 2007 and 2011 a Company in the name and style of Associate D&eacute;cor Limited (hereinafter referred to as 'the Company' for short) is established to be in the business of manufacture of wood products, particle boards, laminates and other wood panel 6 products. Between 2010 and 2015 the Company requested the 1st respondent/Punjab National Bank to grant a term loan and other several credit facilities for the purpose of meeting its capital expenses and working capital requirement. The consortium of Banks i.e., respondents 1, 2 and 3/Punjab National Bank, Union of Bank of India and Bank of Baroda granted about 582 crores to the Company to which the petitioner had executed further term loan agreements by offering huge collaterals as security in favour consortium of Banks. In the year 2016 various disputes arose inter se between the promoters of the Company on account of unexpected changes in the market and Company's operations resulting in irregular loan repayments and categorization of account of the Company in the Banks as a Non Performing Asset ('NPA' for short).</p>
<p title=";}i:2;i:4770;}i:47;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5924;}i:48;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:5925;}i:49;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5929;}i:50;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:5930;}i:51;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5946;}i:52;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:5947;}i:53;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5952;}i:54;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:32:">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:5953;}i:55;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5985;}i:56;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:5986;}i:57;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5990;}i:58;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:5991;}i:59;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6007;}i:60;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:6008;}i:61;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6013;}i:62;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:606:">4. The Banks then instituted recovery proceedings against the Company before the Debt Recovery Tribunal 1 &amp; 2 at Bengaluru. Simultaneously, the 1st respondent/Punjab National Bank files a petition under Section 7 of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 (hereinafter referred to as 'the Code' for short) before the 7 National Company Law Tribunal ('NCLT' for short) seeking initiation of Corporate Insolvency Resolution process ('CIR' for short) against the Company on account of default in repaying the term loan. The application was entertained on the file of NCLT, Bengaluru Bench.</p>
<p title=";}i:2;i:6014;}i:63;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6620;}i:64;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:6621;}i:65;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6625;}i:66;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:6626;}i:67;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6642;}i:68;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:6643;}i:69;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6648;}i:70;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:32:">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:6649;}i:71;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6681;}i:72;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:6682;}i:73;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6686;}i:74;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:6687;}i:75;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6703;}i:76;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:6704;}i:77;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:6709;}i:78;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1019:">5. During the pendency of proceedings before the NCLT, the Company's operations had picked up significantly and monthly turnover in the range of `30/- crores was generated. Therefore, the Directors of the Company began to negotiate with the consortium of Banks for regularization and restructuring of the loan account of the Company. The Corporate Debtor i.e., the Company filed an interlocutory application seeking adjournment of proceedings before the NCLT on the ground that certain amounts had to be realized by the Company which would far exceed the liabilities of the Company to the consortium of Banks. Pending the application, the NCLT appointed an Interim Resolution Professional for the Company under the Code and the petitioner then ceased to be the Director of the Company as respondent No.4 who is appointed as Interim Resolution Professional took over the affairs of 8 the Company. Thereafter, the 4th respondent made it public that the CIR process had commenced with regard to the Company.</p>
<p title=";}i:2;i:6710;}i:79;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7729;}i:80;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:7730;}i:81;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7734;}i:82;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:7735;}i:83;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7751;}i:84;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:7752;}i:85;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7757;}i:86;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:32:">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:7758;}i:87;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7790;}i:88;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:4:"Fact";}i:2;i:7791;}i:89;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7795;}i:90;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:7796;}i:91;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7812;}i:92;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Facts";}i:2;i:7813;}i:93;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:7818;}i:94;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:678:">6. Pursuant to all the aforesaid, a Committee of Creditors of the Company came to be constituted wherein the 1st respondent had 24.16% voting share and 2nd and 3rd respondents had 41.18% and 34.66% voting share respectively. The first meeting of the Committee of Creditors was held on 26-12-2018. The Committee of Creditors then appointed the 4th respondent as a Resolution Professional of the Corporate Debtor, him already being appointed as Interim Resolution Professional. The Resolution Professional issues Form-G through which he invites expssion of interest for submission of resolution plans in respect of the Corporate Debtor. Things go on in this manner.</p>
<p title=";}i:2;i:7819;}i:95;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8497;}i:96;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:8498;}i:97;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8515;}i:98;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:8516;}i:99;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8532;}i:100;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:8533;}i:101;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8543;}i:102;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:32:">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:8544;}i:103;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8576;}i:104;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:8577;}i:105;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8594;}i:106;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:8595;}i:107;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8611;}i:108;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:8612;}i:109;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:8622;}i:110;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1702:">7. On 09-10-2019 the 4th respondent in the 11th meeting of the Committee of Creditors reveals that about 22 prospective resolution applicants had confirmed their interest and draws a provisional list of resolution applicants. Thereafter the 4th respondent issues a request for resolution plan along with updated information memorandum to the prospective resolution applicants. 9 The resolution plans submitted by two of the resolution applicants were discussed and further negotiated in the 19th meeting of the Committee of Creditors. The 19th meeting was originally convened to be held on 07-02-2020. The agenda for the said meeting was also set out for discussion. On 10-02-2022 resolution plans of both resolution applicants were discussed and further negotiated. The resolution plans were scored on evaluation matrix with Archidplay receiving a total score of 57.20 and respondent No.5 receiving 72.64. The next day i.e., 11-02-2020 an e-mail is sent communicating that the 2nd meeting of 19th Committee of Creditors which was sought to be adjourned on 10-02-2020 is scheduled on the same day i.e., 11-02-2020 at 3.00 p.m. The mail was received at 12.20 p.m. Though the earlier resolution resulted in adjournment of proceedings, this was varied at and directed to be done on 11.02.2020 at 3.00 p.m. Respondents 1 to 3 on the said day approved the resolution plan dated 10-02-2020. On 13-02-2020 during the period of CIR process, the petitioner addresses a letter to respondents 1 to 4 expssing the intention to pay `250 crores to clear the outstanding loan amount of the Company if re-structuring 10 or settlement proposal in order to revive the Company would be accepted.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p title=";}i:2;i:8623;}i:111;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10325;}i:112;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:10326;}i:113;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10343;}i:114;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:10344;}i:115;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10360;}i:116;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:10361;}i:117;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10371;}i:118;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:451:">8. Pending all the aforesaid process, the Resolution Professional marches ahead and seeks to recover the assets of the Company. It is then the petitioner knocks at the doors of this Court in the subject petition calling in question certain decision of the Resolution Professional and orders passed by the NCLT accepting the resolution process initiated by respondents 1 to 3 without reconsidering the case of the petitioner.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p title=";}i:2;i:10372;}i:119;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10823;}i:120;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Issue";}i:2;i:10824;}i:121;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10829;}i:122;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:10830;}i:123;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10846;}i:124;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Issue";}i:2;i:10847;}i:125;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10852;}i:126;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:45:">SUBMISSIONS: PETITIONER:</p>
<ul>
<li title=";}i:2;i:10853;}i:127;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10898;}i:128;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:21:"Respondent's Argument";}i:2;i:10899;}i:129;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10920;}i:130;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:10921;}i:131;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10937;}i:132;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:7:"RespArg";}i:2;i:10938;}i:133;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:10945;}i:134;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:677:">9. The learned senior counsel appearing for the petitioner would urge the following contentions; that the petitioner had offered `280 cores as payment to the Committee of Creditors as opposed to `243 crores by the 5th respondent. The petitioner later offered to settle the statutory dues in full amounting to `40 crores as opposed to `40 crores offered by the 5th respondent. The petitioner also offered to settle the dues of operation creditors in 11 full amounting to `60 crores as opposed to `60 crores offered by the 5th respondent, as also, the dues of the workmen in full which was approximately `1 crore which was the same offered by the 5th respondent.</li>
<li title=";}i:2;i:10946;}i:135;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:11623;}i:136;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:21:"Respondent's Argument";}i:2;i:11624;}i:137;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:11645;}i:138;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:11646;}i:139;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:11662;}i:140;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:7:"RespArg";}i:2;i:11663;}i:141;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:11670;}i:142;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1563:">10. The Committee of Creditors rejected the offer of the petitioner without any reason and notified the petitioner that since the offer of the 5th respondent had already been approved, no opportunity can be given for considering the proposal of the petitioner is the submission of the learned senior counsel. It is his emphatic submission that the proposal of the 5th respondent was approved in the meeting of the Committee of Creditors held on 11-02-2020 wherein only three hours notice was given to the petitioner in complete contravention of Section 24 of the Code and Regulation 19 of the IBBI Regulations. It is his submission that on 10-02-2020 deliberation on approval of a particular resolution plan of the 5th respondent was in place. Deliberations happened. The deliberations did not conclude on the said day. There was an amendment to the agenda. Therefore, the matter was taken up on the next day i.e., 11-02-2020 at 3.00 p.m. to discuss the amended 12 agenda. Therefore notice was issued to the petitioner three hours before the said time of the meeting. This is contrary to the Code and the Regulations. It is here the learned senior counsel for the petitioner would submit that there is violation of the principles of natural justice. He would therefore, contend that the said resolution dated 11-02-2020 must be annulled and all the subsequent actions taken must be set at naught, as everything has sprung from the approval of the resolution plan of the 5th respondent.</li>
</ul>
<p>RESPONDENTS 1 TO 3/COMMITTEE OF CREDITORS:</p>
<ul>
<li title=";}i:2;i:11671;}i:143;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:13234;}i:144;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:13235;}i:145;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:13252;}i:146;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:13253;}i:147;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:13269;}i:148;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:13270;}i:149;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:13280;}i:150;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1731:">11. Respondents 1 to 3 would vehemently contend that what the petitioner is now wanting is putting the clock back, which cannot be allowed, as much water has flown beneath the bridge after declaration of the 5th respondent's plan to be an approved resolution plan. The learned senior counsel would submit that after the resolution plan of the 5th respondent was approved, one Svamitva Landmarks had approached the NCLT seeking its plan also to be taken into consideration. The NCLT had allowed the petition filed by Svamitva Landmarks. This was challenged by the 13 Committee of Creditors before the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal ('Appellate Tribunal' for short). The Appellate Tribunal sets the order aside and directs the resolution plan of the 5th respondent to be approved without any loss of time, but within 4 weeks. This was challenged by Svamitva Landmarks before the Apex Court in a civil appeal arising from the order of the Appellate Tribunal. The Apex Court rejected the civil appeal. Therefore, the learned senior counsel would submit that there is merger of the order passed by the Appellate Tribunal with the order of the Apex Court. How the petitioner is affected is that the petitioner had filed an application seeking to implead himself into the proceedings before the Appellate Tribunal. The said impleading application comes to be rejected. Therefore, the learned senior counsel would submit that there is tacit approval and tacit rejection of the claim of the petitioner. Therefore, he would submit that going back to the date of the resolution is impermissible in the light of the proceedings having attained finality by the dismissal of the civil appeal before the Apex court.</li>
</ul>
<p title=";}i:2;i:13281;}i:151;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15012;}i:152;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Issue";}i:2;i:15013;}i:153;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15018;}i:154;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:15019;}i:155;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15035;}i:156;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:5:"Issue";}i:2;i:15036;}i:157;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15041;}i:158;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:36:">5th RESPONDENT:</p>
<ul>
<li title=";}i:2;i:15042;}i:159;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15078;}i:160;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:15079;}i:161;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15096;}i:162;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:15097;}i:163;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15113;}i:164;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:15114;}i:165;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:15124;}i:166;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1288:">12. The learned counsel repsenting the 5th respondent would toe the lines of the Committee of Creditors to contend that it is the commercial wisdom of the Committee of Creditors to approve the resolution plan of the 5th respondent. This Court in exercise of its jurisdiction under Article 226 of the Constitution of India would not entertain a petition, which is a commercial wisdom of approval of resolution plan. Insofar as the opportunity not being granted to the petitioner, the learned senior counsel would contend that the petitioner was repsented on 10-02-2020 on which day the meeting was adjourned to 11-02-2020. It was not a meeting on a new agenda. The agenda was the same. Since the petitioner had already repsented on 10-02-2020 and all the deliberations had happened on 10-02-2020 in the psence of the petitioner, the petitioner cannot complain of violation of principles of natural justice. On 11-02-2020 notice was given to the petitioner and he has also appeared through video conferencing which was always available to him. The learned senior counsel would struck to the stand that it was only an adjourned meeting and there can be no question of another notice being issued for an adjourned meeting. 15 THE 4TH RESPONDENT / THE RESOLUTION PROFESSIONAL:</li>
<li title=";}i:2;i:15125;}i:167;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:16413;}i:168;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:21:"Petitioner's Argument";}i:2;i:16414;}i:169;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:16435;}i:170;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:16436;}i:171;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:16452;}i:172;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:6:"PetArg";}i:2;i:16453;}i:173;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:16459;}i:174;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1540:">13. The learned senior counsel repsenting 4th respondent/Resolution Professional would vehemently contend that the writ petition is not maintainable against the proceedings upnder the Code. The petitioner is trading on two jurisdictions. On the ground that the petitioner is not being afforded an opportunity inter alia had filed an application to implead himself before Appellate Tribunal. He fails. He keeps quiet and files I.A.No.10 before the NCLT, Bangalore. During the pendency of hearing on I.A.No.10 he approaches this Court in the subject petition suppssing the fact that he had pferred an application before the NCLT in I.A.No.10. She would further contend that the petitioner should not be shown any sympathy on imaginary violation of principles of natural justice and would also contend that any order passed in favour of the petitioner would straight away run foul of the order passed by the Appellate Tribunal, which is affirmed by the Apex Court. She would reiterate the submission that it was only an adjourned meeting adjourned from 10-02-2020 to 11-02-2020 and if it is an adjourned meeting, there can be no question of grant of 16 any opportunity to the petitioner. The learned senior counsel would submit that the resolution plan of the 5th respondent comes to be approved in the Committee of Creditors and such a decision is not amenable to judicial review under Article 226 of the Constitution of India and would seek dismissal of the petition. RESPONDENTS 6 TO 9/LEARNED SOLITICITOR GENERAL OF INDIA:</li>
<li title=";}i:2;i:16460;}i:175;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18000;}i:176;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:18001;}i:177;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18018;}i:178;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:18019;}i:179;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18035;}i:180;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:18036;}i:181;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18046;}i:182;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:892:">14. The learned Solicitor General of India repsenting respondents 6 to 9 would lend his support to the contentions of the Committee of Creditors/respondents 1 to 3 in reiterating that the agenda that approved the resolution plan of the 5th respondent was an extended meeting or an adjourned meeting at best and cannot by any way be construed to be a new meeting on a new agenda. Therefore, the requirement of issuance of notice to the petitioner, again for an adjourned agenda, would not be required. He would submit that the petitioner's rights have been determined to his detriment by the Tribunal, while it rejected the impleading application on whatsoever ground it could be. The whole hog of the proceedings are affirmed by the Apex Court and therefore, the 17 petitioner cannot now iterate the contentions that he has lost. He would also seek dismissal of the petition.</li>
<li title=";}i:2;i:18047;}i:183;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18939;}i:184;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:18940;}i:185;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18957;}i:186;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:18958;}i:187;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18974;}i:188;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:18975;}i:189;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:18985;}i:190;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:849:">15. In reply to all these submissions, the learned senior counsel for the petitioner would again take this Court through the documents appended to the petition to demonstrate that, if it was not an amended agenda, there was no question of issuance of notice at all. But, notice is issued to the petitioner and if it is issued then they will have to follow the rigour of the Code and the Regulations. He would further contend that the Resolution Professional has shown interest more than what is necessary. The Resolution Professional ought to have been neutral, as the role of the Resolution Professional is limited to the conduct of proceedings and not to show any partisan attitude towards anybody. The submissions that are made by the Resolution Professional before this Court are clearly an act of overstepping the jurisdiction.</li>
<li title=";}i:2;i:18986;}i:191;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:19835;}i:192;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:17:"Court's Reasoning";}i:2;i:19836;}i:193;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:19853;}i:194;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:16:" data-structure=";}i:2;i:19854;}i:195;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteopening";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:19870;}i:196;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:10:"CDiscource";}i:2;i:19871;}i:197;a:3:{i:0;s:18:"doublequoteclosing";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:19881;}i:198;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:198:">16. I have given my anxious consideration to the submissions made by the learned senior counsel for the respective parties, other counsel and have perused the material on record. 18</li>
